export default "# INTRODUCTION TO LINUX SECURITY\n\n# PART 1 OF 4: LINUX SECURITY FUNDAMENTALS\n\n## 1. What Is Linux Security?\n\nLinux security refers to the practices, technologies and configurations used to protect Linux operating systems, their users, applications and data against unauthorized access, modification, disruption and other threats.\n\nLinux is widely used in:\n\n- Cloud servers.\n- Web hosting infrastructure.\n- Cybersecurity laboratories.\n- Network appliances.\n- Enterprise systems.\n- Android-based devices.\n- Development environments.\n- Supercomputers.\n\nBecause Linux frequently runs critical infrastructure, securing it is an important responsibility for system administrators and cybersecurity professionals.\n\nImagine you operate a Linux VPS hosting a marketplace API.\n\nThe server contains:\n\n- Application source code.\n- Database credentials.\n- Customer information.\n- SSH access.\n- Running application services.\n- Network configurations.\n\nIf an attacker gains unauthorized access, they may attempt to:\n\n1. Read sensitive information.\n2. Steal authentication credentials.\n3. Modify application files.\n4. Install malicious software.\n5. Create unauthorized user accounts.\n6. Establish persistent access.\n7. Disrupt services.\n8. Exfiltrate data.\n\nLinux security aims to prevent these activities, detect them when they occur and support recovery.\n\n## 2. Understanding the Linux Security Model\n\nLinux uses several security mechanisms that work together.\n\nImportant components include:\n\n| Component | Security responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| User accounts | Identify system users |\n| Groups | Organize users and permissions |\n| File permissions | Control access to files and directories |\n| Ownership | Determine who owns system resources |\n| Authentication | Verify user identity |\n| Authorization | Determine what users can do |\n| Process isolation | Separate running processes |\n| Firewall | Control network traffic |\n| Mandatory access control | Enforce additional security policies |\n| Logging | Record system activities |\n| Updates | Fix known vulnerabilities |\n\nThese mechanisms work together.\n\nFor example, when a developer connects to a Linux server using SSH, the system authenticates the developer and determines which operations the account is allowed to perform.\n\nThe developer should not automatically receive unrestricted administrative privileges.\n\n## 3. Understanding the Principle of Least Privilege\n\nThe principle of least privilege states that users and applications should receive only the permissions necessary to perform their intended tasks.\n\nConsider a marketplace server.\n\nYou have three accounts:\n\n| Account | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| `root` | Full system administration |\n| `developer` | Application development and maintenance |\n| `apiuser` | Runs the marketplace API |\n\nThe API process does not need unrestricted control over the operating system.\n\nTherefore, it should run using a dedicated account such as `apiuser`.\n\nIf the application becomes compromised, limiting its privileges can reduce the damage an attacker can cause.\n\n### Why running everything as root is dangerous\n\nThe root account has extensive administrative authority.\n\nIf an application running as root is compromised, the attacker may inherit powerful system privileges.\n\nFor example, an attacker might attempt to:\n\n- Modify system configuration.\n- Access protected files.\n- Create privileged accounts.\n- Change security settings.\n- Install unauthorized services.\n\nRunning applications as non-root users helps reduce this risk.\n\n## 4. Understanding Linux Users and Groups\n\nLinux uses user accounts to associate processes and system actions with identities.\n\nImportant account types include:\n\n- Root user.\n- Regular users.\n- System users.\n- Service accounts.\n\n### Viewing the current user\n\n```bash\nwhoami\n```\n\nThis displays the username associated with your current session.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\ndeveloper\n```\n\n### Viewing user identity information\n\n```bash\nid\n```\n\nExample output:\n\n```text\nuid=1001(developer) gid=1001(developer) groups=1001(developer),27(sudo)\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `uid`: User ID.\n- `gid`: Primary group ID.\n- `groups`: Groups associated with the user.\n\n### Listing users\n\n```bash\ncat /etc/passwd\n```\n\nThis displays account information.\n\nA simplified entry looks like:\n\n```text\ndeveloper:x:1001:1001:Developer:/home/developer:/bin/bash\n```\n\nIts fields represent:\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `developer` | Username |\n| `x` | Password information is stored separately |\n| `1001` | User ID |\n| `1001` | Primary group ID |\n| `Developer` | User description |\n| `/home/developer` | Home directory |\n| `/bin/bash` | Login shell |\n\nThe `/etc/passwd` file is normally readable by ordinary users because it contains account metadata, not password hashes.\n\nPassword hashes are generally stored in `/etc/shadow`, which has restricted access.\n\n### Understanding service accounts\n\nA service account is a user identity created to run a particular application or service.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\nsudo useradd --system --no-create-home --shell /usr/sbin/nologin apiuser\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `sudo` runs the command with administrative privileges.\n- `useradd` creates a user.\n- `--system` creates a system account.\n- `--no-create-home` avoids creating a home directory.\n- `--shell /usr/sbin/nologin` prevents normal interactive login.\n- `apiuser` is the account name.\n\nThe exact location of `nologin` can vary by Linux distribution. Check it with:\n\n```bash\nwhich nologin\n```\n\nA dedicated service account reduces unnecessary access.\n\n## 5. Linux File Permissions\n\nLinux uses file permissions to control who can read, modify or execute files.\n\nThere are three basic permission categories:\n\n| Category | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| User | Owner of the file |\n| Group | Users belonging to the file's group |\n| Others | Everyone else |\n\nThere are also three basic permission types:\n\n| Permission | Symbol | Meaning for files |\n|---|---|---|\n| Read | `r` | View file contents |\n| Write | `w` | Modify file contents |\n| Execute | `x` | Execute the file as a program or script |\n\nFor directories, the meaning differs slightly:\n\n- Read allows listing directory entries.\n- Write allows creating, deleting and renaming entries, subject to other restrictions.\n- Execute allows traversing or accessing entries when other permissions permit it.\n\n### Viewing permissions\n\n```bash\nls -l\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```text\n-rw-r--r-- 1 developer developer 1200 app.js\n```\n\nLet's interpret:\n\n```text\n-rw-r--r--\n```\n\nThe first character indicates the file type.\n\nThe remaining nine characters represent permissions.\n\n```text\nrw-\n```\n\nThe owner can read and write.\n\n```text\nr--\n```\n\nThe group can read.\n\n```text\nr--\n```\n\nOthers can read.\n\n### Understanding numeric permissions\n\nLinux also represents permissions numerically.\n\n| Permission | Numeric value |\n|---|---:|\n| Read | 4 |\n| Write | 2 |\n| Execute | 1 |\n\nThe values are added together.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\n7 = 4 + 2 + 1 = rwx\n6 = 4 + 2 = rw-\n5 = 4 + 1 = r-x\n4 = 4 = r--\n```\n\nConsider:\n\n```bash\nchmod 750 app.sh\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `7`: Owner gets read, write and execute.\n- `5`: Group gets read and execute.\n- `0`: Others receive no permissions.\n\nAnother example:\n\n```bash\nchmod 600 .env\n```\n\nThe owner can read and write the file.\n\nOther users receive no permissions through ordinary Unix permission bits.\n\nThis is useful for protecting sensitive configuration files, although access through privileged processes, backups and other mechanisms must also be considered.\n\n## 6. Ownership and Permission Management\n\nEvery file and directory has an owner and a group.\n\nView ownership:\n\n```bash\nls -l\n```\n\nChange file ownership:\n\n```bash\nsudo chown apiuser:apiuser app.js\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `sudo` provides administrative authority.\n- `chown` changes ownership.\n- `apiuser:apiuser` specifies the owner and group.\n- `app.js` is the target file.\n\nChange permissions:\n\n```bash\nchmod 640 app.js\n```\n\nThis grants:\n\n- Owner: Read and write.\n- Group: Read.\n- Others: No permissions.\n\n### Why permissions matter in cybersecurity\n\nIncorrect permissions may expose sensitive information.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\n-rw-r--r-- .env\n```\n\nIf the file contains database credentials, other local users may be able to read them.\n\nA more restrictive configuration might be:\n\n```text\n-rw------- .env\n```\n\nThis prevents access through ordinary file permission checks by other users.\n\n## 7. Understanding Sudo and Administrative Privileges\n\nLinux uses `sudo` to allow authorized users to execute commands with elevated privileges.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update\n```\n\nThis executes the package index update using administrative privileges.\n\n### Why sudo is safer than always using root\n\nUsing a normal account with controlled `sudo` access:\n\n- Reduces accidental administrative actions.\n- Provides a record of many privileged command executions.\n- Allows administrative permissions to be restricted.\n- Avoids using a permanently privileged interactive session.\n\nView your sudo permissions:\n\n```bash\nsudo -l\n```\n\nThis lists commands your account is permitted to execute through sudo.\n\nThe sudo policy is generally configured through `/etc/sudoers` and files under `/etc/sudoers.d/`.\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\nsudo visudo\n```\n\nto edit the sudo policy safely.\n\nDo not grant unrestricted sudo access to service accounts unless there is a specific and justified requirement.\n\n## 8. Authentication and Password Security\n\nAuthentication is the process of verifying a user's identity.\n\nLinux commonly supports authentication through:\n\n- Passwords.\n- SSH keys.\n- Multi-factor authentication integrations.\n- Centralized identity systems.\n- Other configured authentication mechanisms.\n\n### Understanding password security\n\nWeak passwords make unauthorized access easier.\n\nExamples of weak password choices include:\n\n```text\n123456\npassword\nadmin123\n```\n\nBetter passwords should be:\n\n- Long.\n- Unique.\n- Difficult to guess.\n- Not reused across services.\n\nPassword hashes are stored and verified through configured authentication mechanisms.\n\nModern Linux distributions commonly use password-hashing algorithms such as yescrypt or other supported schemes.\n\nPasswords should never be stored as plain text.\n\n### Viewing password aging information\n\n```bash\nsudo chage -l developer\n```\n\nThis displays password aging information for the account.\n\n### Locking an account\n\n```bash\nsudo passwd -l developer\n```\n\nThis locks password-based authentication for the account through the system's configured password mechanism.\n\nImportant: Locking a password does not necessarily disable every possible login method. SSH keys or other authentication mechanisms may still work unless separately restricted.\n\n### Unlocking an account\n\n```bash\nsudo passwd -u developer\n```\n\nUse this only when you are authorized and have verified that the account should be re-enabled.\n\n## 9. Understanding SSH Security\n\nSSH stands for Secure Shell.\n\nIt provides encrypted remote access to systems.\n\nAdministrators commonly use SSH to manage Linux servers.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\nssh developer@203.0.113.20\n```\n\nThis connects to a server using the account `developer`.\n\nThe IP address is an illustrative documentation address.\n\n### SSH authentication methods\n\nCommon methods include:\n\n- Password authentication.\n- Public-key authentication.\n- Hardware-backed authentication, depending on configuration.\n\n### Understanding SSH keys\n\nSSH public-key authentication uses a key pair:\n\n- Private key.\n- Public key.\n\nThe private key remains with the user and should be protected.\n\nThe public key is placed on the remote server.\n\nGenerate an Ed25519 key pair:\n\n```bash\nssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C \"developer@workstation\"\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `ssh-keygen` generates SSH keys.\n- `-t ed25519` selects the Ed25519 key algorithm.\n- `-C` adds a comment identifying the key.\n\nThe command normally creates:\n\n```text\n~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub\n```\n\nThe first is the private key.\n\nThe second is the public key.\n\nCopy the public key to an authorized server account using a trusted method. Where available, `ssh-copy-id` can help:\n\n```bash\nssh-copy-id developer@SERVER_IP\n```\n\nDo not upload or share the private key.\n\n### SSH hardening\n\nThe SSH server configuration is commonly found at:\n\n```text\n/etc/ssh/sshd_config\n```\n\nImportant settings include:\n\n```text\nPermitRootLogin no\nPubkeyAuthentication yes\nPasswordAuthentication no\n```\n\nThese settings can strengthen SSH access when key-based access has already been configured and tested.\n\n**Critical operational warning:** Do not disable password authentication before confirming that key-based login works in a separate session. Otherwise, you could lock yourself out.\n\nConfiguration names and defaults can vary between OpenSSH versions and distribution configurations. Validate changes before reloading the service.\n\n## 10. Part 1 Practical Exercise\n\nUse a Linux virtual machine or an isolated lab environment.\n\nComplete these tasks:\n\n1. Identify your current user.\n2. Display your user ID and groups.\n3. Inspect `/etc/passwd`.\n4. Create a dedicated service account.\n5. Create a directory named `/opt/secure-app`.\n6. Assign ownership to the service account.\n7. Create a file named `.env`.\n8. Restrict its permissions using `chmod`.\n9. Inspect the final permissions using `ls -l`.\n10. Examine your sudo permissions.\n\nUseful commands:\n\n```bash\nwhoami\nid\ncat /etc/passwd\nsudo useradd --system --no-create-home --shell /usr/sbin/nologin apiuser\nsudo mkdir -p /opt/secure-app\nsudo chown apiuser:apiuser /opt/secure-app\nsudo touch /opt/secure-app/.env\nsudo chmod 600 /opt/secure-app/.env\nls -la /opt/secure-app\nsudo -l\n```\n\n**Expected result:** You should have a dedicated service account, a protected application directory and a restricted configuration file.\n\nProgress: Part 1 of 4\n\n---\n\n# PART 2 OF 4: LINUX SYSTEM HARDENING AND ACCESS CONTROL\n\n## 11. What Is Linux Hardening?\n\nLinux hardening is the process of reducing the number of security weaknesses in a Linux system.\n\nA default installation may contain services, accounts, software packages and configuration settings that are unnecessary for a particular server.\n\nHardening aims to reduce the attack surface.\n\nAn attack surface is the collection of places through which an attacker might attempt to interact with or compromise a system.\n\nFor example, a VPS hosting a marketplace API may not need:\n\n- A desktop environment.\n- Unused file-sharing services.\n- Unnecessary remote access services.\n- Unrestricted database access.\n- Unused administrative accounts.\n\nRemoving or disabling unnecessary components can reduce risk.\n\n## 12. Keeping Linux Updated\n\nSoftware vulnerabilities are discovered regularly.\n\nOperating system maintainers release security updates to address known weaknesses.\n\nAn outdated Linux server may contain vulnerable:\n\n- Kernel components.\n- OpenSSH versions.\n- Web servers.\n- Libraries.\n- Database software.\n- Application dependencies.\n\n### Updating Ubuntu and Debian\n\nRefresh package information:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update\n```\n\nInstall available package updates:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt upgrade\n```\n\nRemove unnecessary packages:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt autoremove\n```\n\n### Understanding each command\n\n`apt update` downloads current package information.\n\nIt does not install upgrades.\n\n`apt upgrade` installs available package updates.\n\n`apt autoremove` removes packages that were automatically installed as dependencies but are no longer required.\n\n### Checking the Linux kernel\n\n```bash\nuname -r\n```\n\nThis displays the running kernel version.\n\n### Why patch management matters\n\nUpdates can fix:\n\n- Privilege escalation vulnerabilities.\n- Remote code execution vulnerabilities.\n- Authentication weaknesses.\n- Information disclosure problems.\n- Denial-of-service vulnerabilities.\n\nProduction systems should use a planned patching process.\n\nBefore important updates:\n\n1. Review the update.\n2. Confirm backups are available.\n3. Consider application compatibility.\n4. Apply updates.\n5. Reboot when required.\n6. Verify services afterward.\n\n## 13. Managing Running Services\n\nLinux services often run in the background.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- SSH.\n- Nginx.\n- Apache.\n- MongoDB.\n- Docker.\n- Redis.\n\nEvery unnecessary service can increase the attack surface.\n\n### Listing active services\n\n```bash\nsystemctl list-units --type=service --state=running\n```\n\nThis lists running systemd services.\n\n### Checking one service\n\n```bash\nsystemctl status ssh\n```\n\nThis displays the status of the SSH service on systems where it uses that service name.\n\nSome distributions use `sshd` instead.\n\n### Stopping a service\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl stop apache2\n```\n\nThis stops Apache on systems using that service name.\n\n### Disabling automatic startup\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl disable apache2\n```\n\nThis prevents the service from being automatically enabled at boot through its normal enablement configuration.\n\nDisabling a service does not necessarily stop it immediately.\n\n### Masking a service\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl mask apache2\n```\n\nMasking makes the service unavailable for normal activation through systemd until it is unmasked.\n\nUse this only when you understand the service's role and dependencies.\n\n**Security principle:** Do not disable a service simply because you do not recognize its name. Investigate its purpose first.\n\n## 14. Managing Network Exposure With Firewalls\n\nA firewall controls which network traffic is allowed or denied.\n\nLinux systems can use firewall frameworks such as:\n\n- Netfilter.\n- nftables.\n- iptables.\n- UFW.\n- firewalld.\n\nUFW is commonly used as a simpler firewall management interface on Ubuntu.\n\n### Checking UFW status\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw status verbose\n```\n\nThis displays firewall status and configured rules.\n\n### Allowing SSH\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw allow 22/tcp\n```\n\nThis permits TCP connections to port 22.\n\nIf SSH uses a different port, adjust the rule accordingly.\n\n### Allowing HTTPS\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw allow 443/tcp\n```\n\nThis permits inbound HTTPS traffic.\n\n### Allowing HTTP\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw allow 80/tcp\n```\n\nThis permits inbound HTTP traffic.\n\n### Enabling UFW\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw enable\n```\n\nBefore enabling a firewall on a remote server, confirm that SSH access is allowed from your trusted management network.\n\nOtherwise, you may lose remote access.\n\n### Restricting SSH access\n\nFor example:\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw allow from 192.168.1.50 to any port 22 proto tcp\n```\n\nThis allows SSH connections from the specified source IP.\n\nThe example assumes that the address represents a trusted administrator host reachable through the server's network path.\n\n### Denying public database access\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw deny 27017/tcp\n```\n\nThis denies inbound TCP traffic to port 27017.\n\nHowever, the correct production approach is to ensure that the database is not publicly exposed and to allow only required internal communication.\n\nFirewall rules should be tested against the actual network architecture.\n\n## 15. Understanding Linux Mandatory Access Control\n\nTraditional Linux permissions use discretionary access control.\n\nThe file owner and privileged administrators can control access according to ordinary permission rules.\n\nMandatory Access Control (MAC) adds another layer of policy enforcement.\n\nTwo important Linux security systems are:\n\n- SELinux.\n- AppArmor.\n\n### SELinux\n\nSecurity-Enhanced Linux provides a security architecture that enforces mandatory access-control policies.\n\nIt can restrict what processes are allowed to access, even when traditional Unix permissions might otherwise permit access.\n\nFor example, a web server process may be prevented from accessing files outside its permitted security context.\n\n### AppArmor\n\nAppArmor applies application-specific security profiles.\n\nA profile can restrict the files, capabilities and other resources that a program may access.\n\nThis helps limit the consequences of a compromised application.\n\n### Why MAC is important\n\nSuppose an attacker exploits a vulnerability in a web application.\n\nTraditional permissions may still allow the process to access certain resources.\n\nA correctly configured AppArmor or SELinux policy can further restrict the process.\n\nNeither system automatically makes a server secure. Policies must be configured, maintained and tested.\n\n## 16. Protecting Sensitive Files\n\nLinux contains files that store sensitive configuration and authentication information.\n\nExamples:\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `/etc/shadow` | Password hashes and aging information |\n| `/etc/passwd` | User account information |\n| `/etc/sudoers` | Sudo authorization policy |\n| `/etc/ssh/sshd_config` | SSH server configuration |\n| `/etc/fstab` | Filesystem mount configuration |\n| Application `.env` files | Application configuration and sometimes secrets |\n\nSensitive files should have appropriate ownership and permissions.\n\n### Inspecting sensitive file permissions\n\n```bash\nls -l /etc/shadow\n```\n\nThe file should have restricted access.\n\n### Inspecting SSH configuration permissions\n\n```bash\nls -l /etc/ssh/sshd_config\n```\n\n### Inspecting environment files\n\n```bash\nls -l /opt/secure-app/.env\n```\n\n### Protecting application secrets\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\nsudo chown apiuser:apiuser /opt/secure-app/.env\nsudo chmod 600 /opt/secure-app/.env\n```\n\nThis assigns ownership to `apiuser` and restricts ordinary access to the file owner.\n\nProduction secret management may require additional controls such as:\n\n- Encrypted secret storage.\n- Restricted deployment permissions.\n- Secret rotation.\n- Audit logging.\n- Avoiding secrets in application logs.\n- Avoiding secrets in source-control repositories.\n\n## 17. Understanding Process Security\n\nA process is a running instance of a program.\n\nEvery Linux process operates with an associated identity and security context.\n\nIf an application runs with unnecessary privileges, compromising it may create additional opportunities for an attacker.\n\n### Viewing processes\n\n```bash\nps aux\n```\n\nThis displays running processes and associated information.\n\n### Searching for a process\n\n```bash\nps aux | grep nginx\n```\n\nThis searches the displayed process list for matching text.\n\n### Understanding process ownership\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nUSER      PID  COMMAND\nroot        1  /sbin/init\nwww-data  520  nginx: worker process\napiuser   740  node server.js\n```\n\nInterpretation:\n\n- `root` owns the initial system process.\n- `www-data` owns an Nginx worker process.\n- `apiuser` owns the marketplace API process.\n\nThe API runs under a dedicated account instead of root.\n\n### Inspecting a process\n\n```bash\nps -o pid,user,group,cmd -p 740\n```\n\nThis displays selected information for process ID 740.\n\nReplace the example PID with the actual process ID.\n\n### Why process ownership matters\n\nIf an application is compromised, its permissions influence what the attacker can do through that process.\n\nRunning applications under separate accounts helps create boundaries between services.\n\n## 18. Linux Kernel Security\n\nThe Linux kernel is the central component that manages hardware, memory, processes and system resources.\n\nKernel security is important because a kernel vulnerability can affect the entire operating system.\n\nSecurity practices include:\n\n- Applying kernel security updates.\n- Removing unnecessary kernel modules where appropriate.\n- Restricting privileged operations.\n- Using security modules such as SELinux or AppArmor.\n- Monitoring suspicious system behaviour.\n- Restricting access to kernel interfaces.\n\n### Viewing loaded kernel modules\n\n```bash\nlsmod\n```\n\nThis displays currently loaded kernel modules.\n\n### Viewing kernel messages\n\n```bash\ndmesg\n```\n\nThis displays kernel ring-buffer messages, subject to system access restrictions.\n\nKernel messages can help investigate:\n\n- Hardware issues.\n- Driver problems.\n- Kernel warnings.\n- Device initialization.\n- Certain security-related events.\n\n## 19. Linux Security Configuration Example\n\nImagine we are hardening a new Ubuntu VPS that hosts a marketplace API.\n\nOur initial security plan:\n\n| Area | Configuration |\n|---|---|\n| Administrative access | SSH restricted to trusted administrators |\n| Application identity | Dedicated non-root user |\n| SSH authentication | Public-key authentication |\n| Firewall | Allow only necessary inbound traffic |\n| Database | Private network access only |\n| Packages | Security updates applied |\n| Services | Unnecessary services disabled |\n| Secrets | Restricted file permissions |\n| Monitoring | Logs and system activity monitored |\n| Backups | Regular protected backups |\n\nThis is a baseline.\n\nA production environment may need additional controls based on its risk profile.\n\n## 20. Part 2 Practical Exercise: Harden a Linux VPS\n\nUse an isolated Linux virtual machine or a server where you have authorization.\n\nComplete these tasks:\n\n1. Inspect the installed Linux distribution.\n2. Check available updates.\n3. Inspect running services.\n4. Identify unnecessary services.\n5. Inspect firewall status.\n6. Configure a firewall to allow required traffic.\n7. Create a dedicated application account.\n8. Restrict access to an application `.env` file.\n9. Inspect SSH configuration.\n10. Review whether AppArmor or SELinux is enabled.\n\nUseful commands:\n\n```bash\ncat /etc/os-release\n\nsudo apt update\n\nsystemctl list-units --type=service --state=running\n\nsudo ufw status verbose\n\nsudo ufw allow 22/tcp\n\nsudo ufw allow 443/tcp\n\nsudo ufw status numbered\n\nsudo aa-status\n```\n\n`aa-status` is available on systems using AppArmor. Systems using SELinux require their corresponding management tools.\n\nDo not enable firewall restrictions or alter SSH authentication on a remote production server without a tested recovery method.\n\nProgress: Part 2 of 4\n\n---\n\n# PART 3 OF 4: LINUX SECURITY MONITORING, THREATS AND DEFENCE\n\n## 21. Why Linux Security Monitoring Matters\n\nHardening reduces security weaknesses, but it cannot guarantee that an attack will never happen.\n\nA secure Linux system must also be monitored.\n\nMonitoring helps identify unusual activities such as:\n\n- Repeated failed login attempts.\n- Unauthorized user creation.\n- Suspicious processes.\n- Unexpected listening ports.\n- Modified system files.\n- Unusual outbound connections.\n- Privilege escalation attempts.\n- Unexpected service restarts.\n\nSecurity monitoring is an important part of defensive cybersecurity.\n\n## 22. Understanding Linux Logs\n\nLinux logs contain records of system and application activity.\n\nThey help administrators understand what happened, when it happened and which services were involved.\n\nImportant log locations include:\n\n| Log location | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `/var/log/auth.log` | Authentication and authorization events on Debian-based systems |\n| `/var/log/syslog` | General system messages on Debian-based systems |\n| `/var/log/kern.log` | Kernel-related messages on some distributions |\n| `/var/log/secure` | Authentication-related events on some Red Hat-based distributions |\n| `/var/log/nginx/` | Nginx access and error logs |\n| `/var/log/apache2/` | Apache logs on many Debian-based systems |\n\nNot every Linux distribution uses exactly the same log locations.\n\nSome systems rely heavily on systemd's journal.\n\n### Viewing authentication logs\n\nOn Ubuntu and many Debian-based systems:\n\n```bash\nsudo tail -f /var/log/auth.log\n```\n\nThis follows new authentication log entries.\n\n### Searching for failed authentication\n\n```bash\nsudo grep -i \"failed\" /var/log/auth.log\n```\n\nThis searches for lines containing `failed`, ignoring case.\n\n### Viewing system logs\n\n```bash\nsudo tail -n 50 /var/log/syslog\n```\n\nThis displays the latest 50 lines of the system log.\n\n### Viewing systemd logs\n\n```bash\nsudo journalctl\n```\n\nThis displays the system journal.\n\nTo inspect SSH service logs:\n\n```bash\nsudo journalctl -u ssh\n```\n\nOn some systems, the service may be named `sshd`.\n\n### Viewing recent logs\n\n```bash\nsudo journalctl -n 50\n```\n\nThis displays the latest 50 journal entries.\n\n### Following logs\n\n```bash\nsudo journalctl -f\n```\n\nThis follows new journal messages.\n\n## 23. Detecting Suspicious Login Attempts\n\nRepeated failed authentication attempts may indicate:\n\n- Incorrectly configured applications.\n- Users entering incorrect credentials.\n- Automated password guessing.\n- Brute-force attempts.\n- Unauthorized access attempts.\n\nA security analyst should examine the source addresses, timestamps, targeted accounts and surrounding events.\n\nExample log pattern:\n\n```text\nFailed password for invalid user admin from 203.0.113.50\nFailed password for root from 203.0.113.50\nFailed password for developer from 203.0.113.50\n```\n\nThis is an illustrative log example.\n\nIt shows repeated failed SSH authentication attempts from the same source.\n\n### Investigating authentication failures\n\n```bash\nsudo grep \"Failed password\" /var/log/auth.log\n```\n\nCount matching entries:\n\n```bash\nsudo grep -c \"Failed password\" /var/log/auth.log\n```\n\nThis counts the matching lines.\n\n### Viewing successful logins\n\n```bash\nlast\n```\n\nThis displays login history from the system's accounting records.\n\n### Viewing failed login records\n\n```bash\nsudo lastb\n```\n\nThis displays failed login attempts where the system maintains the relevant accounting file.\n\nThe output and availability depend on system configuration and log retention.\n\n## 24. Understanding Brute-Force Attacks\n\nA brute-force attack involves repeatedly attempting credentials until a valid combination is found.\n\nFor SSH, an attacker may try many usernames and passwords.\n\nCommon defensive measures include:\n\n- SSH public-key authentication.\n- Disabling direct root login.\n- Strong authentication.\n- Restricting SSH access by source IP.\n- Rate limiting.\n- Multi-factor authentication where supported.\n- Monitoring authentication logs.\n- Keeping OpenSSH updated.\n\n### Understanding Fail2ban\n\nFail2ban is a security tool that monitors logs and can temporarily block IP addresses that generate repeated suspicious authentication failures.\n\nIt uses configured filters and actions.\n\nFor example, an SSH jail can detect repeated failed SSH login attempts and trigger a firewall ban.\n\nFail2ban does not replace strong authentication or proper firewall configuration.\n\nIts effectiveness depends on correct log paths, filters, jail settings and firewall integration.\n\n## 25. Understanding Malware and Persistence on Linux\n\nLinux systems can be affected by malicious software.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Remote access trojans.\n- Cryptominers.\n- Rootkits.\n- Backdoors.\n- Malicious scripts.\n- Credential-stealing tools.\n- Web shells.\n\nAn attacker who gains access may attempt to maintain persistence.\n\nPersistence means keeping access to a compromised system even after some of the original access conditions change.\n\n### Common persistence locations\n\nAn investigator may examine:\n\n| Location | Why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| Cron jobs | May run malicious scripts periodically |\n| systemd services | May automatically start unauthorized programs |\n| SSH authorized keys | May contain unauthorized public keys |\n| Shell startup files | May execute commands during login |\n| Startup scripts | May launch unauthorized programs |\n| User accounts | May include accounts created by an attacker |\n\n### Inspect scheduled tasks\n\n```bash\ncrontab -l\n```\n\nThis displays the current user's scheduled cron jobs.\n\nFor root's cron jobs:\n\n```bash\nsudo crontab -l\n```\n\nSystem-wide cron configuration can also be found in:\n\n```text\n/etc/crontab\n/etc/cron.d/\n/etc/cron.daily/\n/etc/cron.hourly/\n```\n\n### Inspect systemd services\n\n```bash\nsystemctl list-unit-files --type=service\n```\n\nThis lists service unit files and their enablement states.\n\nInvestigate unfamiliar services rather than assuming they are malicious.\n\n### Inspect SSH authorized keys\n\n```bash\ncat ~/.ssh/authorized_keys\n```\n\nThis displays the public keys authorized for the current account.\n\nOnly do this for accounts you are permitted to inspect.\n\nUnexpected keys should be investigated and verified before removal.\n\n## 26. Understanding Rootkits\n\nA rootkit is a type of malicious software designed to hide its presence or maintain privileged access.\n\nSome rootkits attempt to manipulate system behaviour so that malicious processes, files or network activity are difficult to detect.\n\nRootkits may operate at different levels:\n\n- User space.\n- Kernel space.\n- Boot or firmware level.\n\n### Why rootkits are dangerous\n\nThey can undermine the reliability of normal system inspection tools.\n\nFor example, a compromised system may hide a process from standard process listings.\n\nThis means that information collected from a suspected compromised host may not be trustworthy.\n\n### Defensive measures\n\n- Keep the operating system updated.\n- Restrict administrative privileges.\n- Use secure boot where appropriate.\n- Monitor file integrity.\n- Use trusted security tools.\n- Maintain protected backups.\n- Investigate suspicious kernel activity.\n- Rebuild severely compromised systems from trusted installation media when appropriate.\n\n## 27. Monitoring Listening Ports\n\nUnexpected network services may create opportunities for unauthorized access.\n\nLinux provides tools for inspecting listening sockets.\n\n### Using ss\n\n```bash\nsudo ss -tulpen\n```\n\nThe options mean:\n\n- `-t`: TCP sockets.\n- `-u`: UDP sockets.\n- `-l`: Listening sockets.\n- `-p`: Show associated processes where permitted.\n- `-e`: Show extended information.\n- `-n`: Display numeric addresses and ports.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nNetid  State   Local Address:Port\ntcp    LISTEN  0.0.0.0:22\ntcp    LISTEN  0.0.0.0:443\ntcp    LISTEN  127.0.0.1:27017\n```\n\nInterpretation:\n\n- SSH listens on all IPv4 interfaces.\n- HTTPS listens on all IPv4 interfaces.\n- MongoDB listens only on the loopback interface.\n\nBinding a database to `127.0.0.1` prevents direct remote access through that network socket, but other security controls are still necessary.\n\n### Why listening ports matter\n\nAn administrator should know which services are accessible and whether they need to be exposed.\n\nUnexpected listening services should be investigated.\n\nDo not assume that every open port is malicious.\n\n## 28. File Integrity Monitoring\n\nFile integrity monitoring helps detect unexpected changes to important system files.\n\nFor example, an attacker might modify:\n\n```text\n/etc/ssh/sshd_config\n```\n\nor alter an application executable.\n\nMonitoring file changes can help detect suspicious activity.\n\n### Using checksums\n\nA cryptographic hash can help identify whether file contents have changed.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\nsha256sum app.js\n```\n\nThis calculates the SHA-256 hash of the file.\n\nExample output:\n\n```text\na1b2c3d4...  app.js\n```\n\nIf the file changes, its hash will generally change.\n\n### Comparing hashes\n\nGenerate a baseline:\n\n```bash\nsha256sum app.js > app.js.sha256\n```\n\nLater, verify:\n\n```bash\nsha256sum -c app.js.sha256\n```\n\nPossible output:\n\n```text\napp.js: OK\n```\n\nThis indicates that the file matches the stored checksum.\n\nHowever, if an attacker can modify both the application file and the checksum file, they may defeat this basic check.\n\nStore integrity baselines securely, preferably outside the monitored system.\n\n### Advanced integrity monitoring\n\nTools such as AIDE can monitor changes to important files and directories.\n\nThey can help identify unexpected modifications to system configuration and binaries.\n\n## 29. Understanding Linux Auditing\n\nLinux auditing records selected security-relevant system events.\n\nThe Linux Audit framework can help monitor:\n\n- Authentication events.\n- File access.\n- System calls.\n- Configuration changes.\n- Administrative activity.\n\nThe `auditd` service is commonly used to manage audit records on supported Linux systems.\n\n### Checking audit service status\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl status auditd\n```\n\nThis checks whether the audit service is running.\n\n### Searching audit records\n\n```bash\nsudo ausearch -m USER_LOGIN\n```\n\nThis searches for user login events where the corresponding audit records exist.\n\nAudit configuration should be planned carefully because excessive event collection can generate large volumes of logs.\n\n## 30. Understanding Intrusion Detection\n\nAn Intrusion Detection System (IDS) monitors activity and identifies potentially malicious behaviour.\n\nTwo broad categories are:\n\n**Host-based IDS (HIDS)**\n\nMonitors activity on an individual system.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- File integrity monitoring.\n- Host log monitoring.\n- Audit event monitoring.\n\n**Network-based IDS (NIDS)**\n\nMonitors network traffic to identify suspicious communication.\n\nExamples of tools include:\n\n- Snort.\n- Suricata.\n\nA simplified security monitoring architecture:\n\n```text\nLinux Server\n     |\n     v\nSystem Logs and Events\n     |\n     v\nHost Monitoring\n     |\n     v\nSecurity Analysis\n     |\n     v\nAlerts and Investigation\n```\n\nNetwork monitoring can be integrated into the same security operations workflow.\n\nAn IDS detects suspicious activity. An Intrusion Prevention System (IPS) can also take action to block or disrupt certain detected activity.\n\n## 31. Understanding Backups and Recovery\n\nSecurity is not only about preventing attacks.\n\nIt is also about recovering when something goes wrong.\n\nA Linux server should have a backup and recovery strategy.\n\nImportant data may include:\n\n- Application files.\n- Database data.\n- Configuration files.\n- User uploads.\n- Certificates and relevant service configuration.\n- Infrastructure deployment definitions.\n\n### Example: Backing up a directory\n\n```bash\nsudo tar -czf app-backup.tar.gz /opt/secure-app\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `tar` creates an archive.\n- `-c` creates a new archive.\n- `-z` compresses it using gzip.\n- `-f` specifies the output file.\n- `app-backup.tar.gz` is the backup filename.\n- `/opt/secure-app` is the directory being archived.\n\n### Inspecting the archive\n\n```bash\ntar -tzf app-backup.tar.gz\n```\n\nThis lists the archive contents.\n\n### Important backup security practices\n\n- Encrypt sensitive backups.\n- Restrict backup access.\n- Store copies separately from the production server.\n- Test restoration regularly.\n- Maintain multiple recovery points.\n- Protect backups against unauthorized deletion.\n- Include databases using appropriate database-aware backup methods.\n\nA compressed directory archive is not automatically a consistent database backup. Use database-specific backup mechanisms when backing up active databases.\n\n## 32. Part 3 Practical Exercise: Investigate a Linux System\n\nAssume you are a security analyst investigating an Ubuntu server.\n\nComplete these activities:\n\n### Task 1: Inspect authentication events\n\n```bash\nsudo tail -n 100 /var/log/auth.log\n```\n\nIdentify unsuccessful login attempts.\n\n### Task 2: Review login history\n\n```bash\nlast\n```\n\nIdentify recent login sessions.\n\n### Task 3: Inspect running processes\n\n```bash\nps aux\n```\n\nIdentify processes running under unexpected users.\n\n### Task 4: Inspect listening ports\n\n```bash\nsudo ss -tulpen\n```\n\nIdentify exposed services.\n\n### Task 5: Review scheduled tasks\n\n```bash\ncrontab -l\n```\n\nInspect the current user's scheduled tasks.\n\n### Task 6: Inspect systemd services\n\n```bash\nsystemctl list-units --type=service --state=running\n```\n\nInvestigate unfamiliar services.\n\n### Task 7: Calculate an application file hash\n\n```bash\nsha256sum app.js\n```\n\nRecord the checksum.\n\n### Task 8: Inspect system journal events\n\n```bash\nsudo journalctl -p warning -n 50\n```\n\nThis displays recent warning-level and more severe journal entries.\n\n### Task 9: Check firewall rules\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw status verbose\n```\n\nIdentify which inbound connections are permitted.\n\n### Task 10: Write an investigation report\n\nYour report should include:\n\n- System information.\n- Authentication observations.\n- Running processes.\n- Listening ports.\n- Scheduled tasks.\n- Unusual events.\n- Potential security weaknesses.\n- Recommended defensive actions.\n\nProgress: Part 3 of 4\n\n---\n\n# PART 4 OF 4: KAI ASSESSMENT — INTRODUCTION TO LINUX SECURITY\n\n## 33. Assessment Instructions\n\nThis assessment evaluates your understanding of Linux security principles, hardening, monitoring and incident investigation.\n\nAnswer all questions.\n\nFor practical questions, explain what each command does and why it is relevant.\n\n## SECTION A: MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS\n\n**Question 1**\n\nWhat is the main purpose of Linux security?\n\nA. To make the operating system look attractive.\n\nB. To protect the operating system, applications, users and data.\n\nC. To increase screen resolution.\n\nD. To remove all system services.\n\n**Question 2**\n\nWhat does the principle of least privilege mean?\n\nA. Every user should have root access.\n\nB. Users should receive only the permissions necessary for their responsibilities.\n\nC. All files should be publicly accessible.\n\nD. Every application should run as root.\n\n**Question 3**\n\nWhich Linux account has unrestricted administrative authority by default?\n\nA. guest.\n\nB. developer.\n\nC. root.\n\nD. www-data.\n\n**Question 4**\n\nWhat does the Linux command `chmod` manage?\n\nA. File permissions.\n\nB. Network routes.\n\nC. User passwords.\n\nD. Running processes.\n\n**Question 5**\n\nWhich command displays the current user's identity?\n\nA. `whoami`\n\nB. `mkdir`\n\nC. `pwd`\n\nD. `touch`\n\n**Question 6**\n\nWhat is the main purpose of SSH?\n\nA. Encrypting files stored on disk.\n\nB. Secure remote communication and administration.\n\nC. Creating Linux users.\n\nD. Managing database tables.\n\n**Question 7**\n\nWhich command displays running processes?\n\nA. `ps aux`\n\nB. `chmod`\n\nC. `chown`\n\nD. `dig`\n\n**Question 8**\n\nWhat is Linux system hardening?\n\nA. Increasing the number of installed services.\n\nB. Reducing security weaknesses and unnecessary exposure.\n\nC. Disabling all authentication.\n\nD. Removing every user account.\n\n**Question 9**\n\nWhich tool provides an application-specific mandatory access-control system?\n\nA. AppArmor.\n\nB. Nmap.\n\nC. Wireshark.\n\nD. Curl.\n\n**Question 10**\n\nWhat is the purpose of Linux authentication logs?\n\nA. To store website images.\n\nB. To record authentication-related events.\n\nC. To compile application code.\n\nD. To configure network interfaces.\n\n## SECTION B: SHORT-ANSWER QUESTIONS\n\n**Question 11**\n\nDefine Linux security and explain why it is important for cloud servers.\n\n**Question 12**\n\nExplain the difference between authentication and authorization.\n\n**Question 13**\n\nWhat is the principle of least privilege? Give a practical example involving a marketplace API.\n\n**Question 14**\n\nDifferentiate between regular users, system users and the root user.\n\n**Question 15**\n\nExplain Linux file permissions and the meaning of read, write and execute.\n\n**Question 16**\n\nWhat is the difference between `chmod`, `chown` and `chgrp`?\n\n**Question 17**\n\nWhy is it dangerous to run every application as root?\n\n**Question 18**\n\nExplain why SSH key-based authentication can improve server security.\n\n**Question 19**\n\nWhat is Linux hardening, and identify five hardening practices.\n\n**Question 20**\n\nDifferentiate between discretionary access control and mandatory access control.\n\n## SECTION C: COMMAND-BASED QUESTIONS\n\n**Question 21**\n\nWrite a command that displays the current Linux username.\n\n**Question 22**\n\nWrite a command that displays the current user's UID, GID and groups.\n\n**Question 23**\n\nWrite a command that creates a system account named `apiuser` without an interactive login shell.\n\n**Question 24**\n\nWrite a command that changes ownership of `/opt/secure-app` to `apiuser`.\n\n**Question 25**\n\nWrite a command that restricts a `.env` file so that only its owner has read and write permissions.\n\n**Question 26**\n\nWrite a command that displays the currently running systemd services.\n\n**Question 27**\n\nWrite a command that displays listening TCP and UDP sockets together with process information where permitted.\n\n**Question 28**\n\nWrite a command that displays recent authentication events from the system journal.\n\n**Question 29**\n\nWrite a command that displays the current firewall status using UFW.\n\n**Question 30**\n\nWrite a command that calculates the SHA-256 hash of `app.js`.\n\n## SECTION D: SECURITY MONITORING AND TROUBLESHOOTING\n\n**Question 31**\n\nYour Linux VPS contains an application running as root.\n\nExplain the risks and describe how you would migrate it to a dedicated service account.\n\n**Question 32**\n\nYour SSH logs show repeated failed login attempts from an unfamiliar IP address.\n\nExplain how you would investigate and protect the server.\n\n**Question 33**\n\nYour application directory contains a `.env` file with database credentials, and its permissions allow all local users to read it.\n\nExplain the risk and provide a corrected permission configuration.\n\n**Question 34**\n\nYou discover an unfamiliar systemd service that starts automatically.\n\nExplain how you would investigate whether it is legitimate or suspicious.\n\n**Question 35**\n\nYour server has several open ports that are not required by the application.\n\nExplain how you would identify the services and reduce unnecessary exposure.\n\n**Question 36**\n\nYou suspect an attacker has added an unauthorized SSH public key to a server.\n\nExplain how you would investigate the account and respond safely.\n\n**Question 37**\n\nExplain how AppArmor or SELinux can help limit the impact of a compromised application.\n\n**Question 38**\n\nA system has not received security updates for several months.\n\nExplain the potential risks and describe a safe patch-management process.\n\n## SECTION E: PRACTICAL SECURITY SCENARIOS\n\n**Question 39: Securing an API server**\n\nYou have been assigned a Linux VPS hosting a marketplace API.\n\nDesign a basic security configuration covering:\n\n- User accounts.\n- SSH access.\n- Firewall rules.\n- Application permissions.\n- Database access.\n- System updates.\n- Logging.\n\nExplain why each control is necessary.\n\n**Question 40: Investigating suspicious activity**\n\nYou notice the following observations:\n\n```text\nRepeated failed SSH login attempts\nAn unfamiliar listening TCP port\nAn unexpected scheduled cron job\nA newly created user account\nUnusual outbound network connections\n```\n\nExplain how you would investigate each observation.\n\nIdentify the evidence you would collect before taking action.\n\n**Question 41: File integrity investigation**\n\nYou recorded the SHA-256 checksum of an important application file last week.\n\nToday, the checksum is different.\n\nExplain:\n\n- What this change could mean.\n- How you would investigate it.\n- Why a checksum change does not automatically prove an attack.\n- How you would protect file integrity baselines.\n\n**Question 42: Recovery from compromise**\n\nYou suspect that a production Linux server has been compromised.\n\nExplain the response process, including:\n\n1. Initial investigation.\n2. Evidence preservation.\n3. Containment.\n4. Credential rotation.\n5. Eradication.\n6. System rebuilding where necessary.\n7. Restoration from trusted backups.\n8. Post-incident monitoring.\n\n## SECTION F: FINAL PRACTICAL PROJECT\n\n### Project: Linux Server Security Hardening and Monitoring\n\nYour task is to secure a Linux server intended to host a marketplace API.\n\nUse an isolated Linux virtual machine or an authorized test VPS.\n\n### Project requirements\n\n| Area | Practical requirement |\n|---|---|\n| User management | Create a dedicated API service account |\n| Permissions | Apply appropriate file and directory permissions |\n| Authentication | Configure and test SSH access securely |\n| Privilege management | Review sudo permissions |\n| Firewall | Allow only required network traffic |\n| Updates | Apply available security updates |\n| Services | Identify unnecessary running services |\n| Process security | Run the API without unnecessary privileges |\n| Logging | Inspect authentication and system logs |\n| Monitoring | Identify listening ports and unusual activity |\n| Integrity | Create and verify file checksums |\n| Recovery | Prepare a backup and recovery procedure |\n\n### Practical project stages\n\n**Stage 1: System assessment**\n\nCollect:\n\n- Linux distribution and version.\n- Kernel version.\n- Current users.\n- Running services.\n- Listening ports.\n- Firewall status.\n\n**Stage 2: Account security**\n\n- Create a dedicated API service account.\n- Review existing user accounts.\n- Restrict unnecessary administrative access.\n- Review sudo permissions.\n\n**Stage 3: File security**\n\n- Create a protected application directory.\n- Configure ownership.\n- Restrict `.env` permissions.\n- Verify the final access settings.\n\n**Stage 4: SSH security**\n\n- Generate an SSH key pair.\n- Install the public key on the authorized server account.\n- Test key-based access.\n- Review root login and password authentication settings.\n- Apply changes only after verifying that access remains available.\n\n**Stage 5: Network security**\n\n- Configure firewall rules.\n- Allow required application traffic.\n- Restrict SSH to trusted sources where practical.\n- Ensure the database is not publicly exposed.\n\n**Stage 6: Monitoring**\n\n- Inspect authentication logs.\n- Inspect running processes.\n- Inspect listening ports.\n- Review scheduled tasks.\n- Identify unusual services.\n\n**Stage 7: Integrity and recovery**\n\n- Generate SHA-256 checksums for important application files.\n- Store the baseline securely.\n- Create a backup.\n- Test that the backup can be inspected or restored.\n\n### Project evaluation\n\n| Assessment area | Marks |\n|---|---:|\n| Linux security assessment | 10 |\n| User and privilege management | 15 |\n| File and directory permissions | 10 |\n| SSH security | 15 |\n| Firewall and network security | 15 |\n| Security monitoring | 15 |\n| File integrity and backups | 10 |\n| Investigation report | 10 |\n| **Total** | **100** |\n\n## 34. Lesson Completion Checklist\n\nBefore completing this lesson, make sure you can:\n\n- [ ] Explain Linux security.\n- [ ] Understand authentication and authorization.\n- [ ] Apply the principle of least privilege.\n- [ ] Manage Linux users and groups.\n- [ ] Understand file ownership and permissions.\n- [ ] Use `chmod`, `chown` and `chgrp`.\n- [ ] Understand sudo and administrative privileges.\n- [ ] Explain SSH key-based authentication.\n- [ ] Harden SSH configurations safely.\n- [ ] Update Linux packages.\n- [ ] Inspect and manage system services.\n- [ ] Configure firewall rules.\n- [ ] Understand AppArmor and SELinux.\n- [ ] Inspect running processes.\n- [ ] Examine authentication logs.\n- [ ] Identify suspicious login attempts.\n- [ ] Investigate scheduled tasks and persistence.\n- [ ] Monitor listening ports.\n- [ ] Verify file integrity.\n- [ ] Understand Linux auditing and intrusion detection.\n- [ ] Prepare backups and recovery procedures.\n\n## 35. Final Submission to Kai\n\nSubmit your answers to Questions 1–42 together with your practical Linux security project.\n\nKai should evaluate:\n\n1. Understanding of Linux security principles.\n2. User and permission management.\n3. Administrative privilege management.\n4. SSH security knowledge.\n5. Linux hardening skills.\n6. Firewall configuration.\n7. Security monitoring.\n8. Threat identification.\n9. Investigation and incident response.\n10. Practical system administration skills.\n\nKai should mark each answer, explain incorrect responses and provide additional exercises for topics that need improvement.\n\n**Part 4 of 4 — Lesson Complete**\n\n### Next Recommended Lesson\n\n**Linux Security: Users, Privileges and Access Control**\n\nThis next lesson can go deeper into Linux privilege boundaries, sudo policies, file access controls, special permissions, service isolation and privilege escalation prevention.";
